# web/vendor/ holds byte-exact copies of the extension's shipped UI, and
# check-ui-vendor.mjs authenticates those exact bytes against the committed
# hash manifest (which the Docker build runs too). Git must never normalize
# line endings here: the source files carry mixed EOLs, and a normalized blob
# checks out with different bytes than sync-ui hashed — which is invisible on
# the machine that ran sync-ui and breaks every fresh clone (Railway).
web/vendor/** -text

# Hashbang scripts must check out with LF, even on Windows (core.autocrlf=true).
# Vite strips a leading `#!...` with a regex that only matches a LF terminator,
# so a CRLF checkout leaves the `#!` in the JS it hands the parser and the file
# dies as `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` — reported against whatever
# imported it, because the transformed module carries no usable source position
# (vitejs/vite#23034). Node, `node --check` and esbuild all accept the same file,
# and Linux CI checks out the LF blob and passes, so this reproduces ONLY on a
# Windows working tree and looks like a corrupt dependency rather than an EOL.
# Cost us a long hunt on scripts/lifecycle-e2e.mjs.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Shell scripts run INSIDE a Linux container. CRLF makes the shebang line read
# as `/usr/bin/env bash`, which does not exist, and the container dies with a
# "bad interpreter" that names a binary you can see is installed. Invisible on
# the machine that wrote the file; certain on every fresh clone.
*.sh text eol=lf
